    <node id="&quot;OMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OMS stands for Organização Mundial de Saúde (World Health Organization), which is an international organization that sets policies and standards for global health."&lt;SEP&gt;"The World Health Organization (OMS) is an international organization responsible for global health policies and regulations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The World Health Organization's division of epidemiology and health statistics."&lt;SEP&gt;Here is a comprehensive summary of the data provided:

**Entities: "OMS" (World Health Organization)**

The World Health Organization, commonly referred to as OMS, is an international organization that plays a crucial role in promoting global health and preventing chronic diseases worldwide. As an international agency responsible for coordinating global health efforts, OMS provides guidelines and recommendations for maintaining good health, public health policies, and healthcare practices.

OMS emphasizes the importance of nutrition, recommending daily consumption of fruits, vegetables, and other essential nutrients. The organization also conducts research on subnutrition, evaluating the impact of vitamin A supplementation in various developing countries.

In addition to its focus on preventive measures, OMS is involved in efforts to control tobacco consumption and has published documents recognizing the benefits of breastfeeding. The organization highlights the importance of community-based care and peer education in supported self-care and recommends the use of acupuncture as a complementary therapy in healthcare.

OMS also provides guidance on palliative care, defining it as an approach to improve quality of life for patients and families facing life-threatening illnesses. The organization has reviewed evidence on the long-term effects of breastfeeding and publishes reviews on its benefits.

In terms of health-related policies, OMS recommends a reduction in caloric intake for obese individuals and provides guidelines for energy needs. The organization also uses the term "displasia" in their guidelines, but notes that it is no longer recommended due to its outdated nature.

Overall, OMS is a global health authority that sets standards and recommendations for various health-related issues, making it an essential resource for healthcare professionals, policymakers, and individuals worldwide.</data>
